syntax: zsh's short form of if with an empty body

Unless SHORT_LOOPS is unset, zsh takes the list after "if" or "elif" up to
the first token that cannot continue it as the condition. When that token
is neither "then" nor "{", the branch is the short form, whose body is a
single sublist that may also be empty, so that whatever closes an
enclosing construct ends the "if" as well. zsh accepts and runs, with
both commands as the condition and no body:

	{ if a; b; }
	f() { if [[ -n $x ]]; then :; else if c && d; e; fi; }

whereas "if a; b; fi" at the top level is still an error at "fi", and
so is an "else" right after a short branch, which zsh attaches to the
"if" and then fails on because it expects "fi" straight away. The end of
a command or process substitution does not end the "if" either, since zsh
parses the substitution on its own and finds the end of the input there:
"$(if a)" fails as "if a" does, while "$( (if a) )" parses. The empty
condition of `{ if }` parses too, as with zsh -n.

Reproducer, before this change:

	$ printf '{ if a; b; }\n' | shfmt -ln zsh
	<standard input>:1:12: `}` can only be used to close a block

With LangZsh, a condition list now also ends at a reserved word which
closes a compound command, IfClause.Short may come with an empty Then,
and IfClause.End falls back to the end of the condition. The parser
tracks whether a ')' closes a substitution rather than a subshell. A closing
reserved word right after a statement is reported as such rather than as
a missing separator.

Part of https://github.com/mvdan/sh/issues/120.

